First, let x be the number of cherry danishes. Thus we can define the relation between the number ofapps cheese danishes and the number of cherry danishes as follows,
$\frac{2}{3}x+3< x< 2x$</think>
 <answer> Next, let us consider the first inequality $\frac{2}{3}x+3< x$. Since we are trying to find a bound which is the smallest possible value, we can find the critical value for x by setting the inequality as an equality.
\begin{align*} \frac{2}{3}x + 3 = x \Rightarrow \frac{1}{3}x=3 \Rightarrow x=9 \end{align*}
Thus, we can assume the number of cherry danishes is at least $9$. Likewise, for the next set of inequalities, we can define the equivalent equality.
\begin{align*} x=2x \Rightarrow 1x=2x \end{align*}
As we can see $1x=2x$ is not true for all values of x, so this set of inequalities has no solution. Therefore, we can ignore this set as we are seeking for the smallest possible value for the total number of danishes.
Next, set the total number of danishes (cheese + cherry) to be 13.
\begin{align*} 9+(13-9) = 13 \end{align*}
Thus, the smallest possible value for the total number of danishes he brings is $\boxed{13}$ </answer>

